From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 19:16:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] fix(gastown): make a patrol cycle burn only the wisp it
 actually holds
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Consolidates the wisp-lifecycle work that was previously spread across three
commits, each fixing the previous one's blast radius. Replaces them with one
statement of the invariant and one shape applied at every site.

THE INVARIANT

  A cycle may burn only a wisp it actually holds — $GC_BEAD_ID, or one it
  resolved as `in_progress`. A wisp in `open` is NOT held. It is live work
  queued for the claim protocol, and the only correct response to "I hold
  nothing and a wisp is queued" is to pour nothing, burn nothing, and let
  `gc hook` claim it.

WHAT THE SUPERSEDED COMMITS GOT WRONG

Reconciling queued wisps before pouring (correct, kept) was introduced without
any notion of which wisp the cycle owns, so a cycle could elect its own wisp as
its successor and then burn it. The follow-up fixed that by excluding
$CURRENT_WISP (correct, kept) but ALSO widened $CURRENT_WISP resolution from
`in_progress` to `in_progress,open` so a never-claimed wisp "still resolves".

Every one of those sites ends in an unconditional `gc bd mol burn
"$CURRENT_WISP"`. The widening therefore turned "adopt the queued wisp as your
successor" into "adopt the queued wisp and destroy it":

  GC_BEAD_ID is exported only by the convergence runner, so it is empty in
  exactly the fresh sessions these fallbacks exist to serve. With one open,
  assigned, never-claimed wisp W1 and nothing in progress:

    CURRENT_WISP <- W1        (widening admits `open`)
    OPEN_WISPS   <- {} minus W1, empty
    NEXT empty   -> pour W2, assign W2
    burn CURRENT_WISP         -> W1 destroyed, no work ever ran
    exit -> W2 open+assigned -> namedWorkReady re-wakes the session -> repeat

  One burn, one pour, zero patrol work, ~40s per cycle, stable at exactly one
  wisp so it reads as a healthy queue to anything counting depth. It is also an
  attractor: once in "queued but never claimed", every wake burns the thing it
  was supposed to pick up, so the state cannot self-clear. Before the widening
  the same state merely stalled non-destructively.

A third commit diagnosed this exactly and wrote the rule down -- "resolve from
in_progress ONLY -- never in_progress,open ... step 3 burns unconditionally" --
then applied it to mol-witness-patrol.toml alone, leaving the other ten sites
widened.

WHAT THIS COMMIT DOES

- All 10 CURRENT_WISP sites + witness THIS_WISP resolve from `in_progress`
  only, unbounded and oldest-first for determinism.
- The held wisp stays excluded from successor candidates.
- Queued wisps reconcile to exactly one; surplus is burned.
- "Hold nothing, one queued" is handled explicitly everywhere. In the deacon
  and witness prompts the existing three-branch shape already handles it by
  falling through to `gc hook`; the widening was the only thing defeating it,
  and a state table now records why no branch may claim it. The refinery and
  formula sites get an explicit stop.
- The reconcile query fails CLOSED. It previously could not distinguish a
  failed lookup from an empty queue, so any transient error poured another
  wisp -- reaching the unbounded accumulation the first commit set out to
  prevent, through its own error path.
- boot's watchdog gains a wisp-visible view ALONGSIDE its original query,
  which is restored verbatim. Widening is safe there and only there, because
  boot reads and never burns.

Verified: all gastown formulas re-parse; gascity's examples/gastown suite run
against these packs via a local replace fails exactly the 13 tests that
pristine upstream f69ec02 already fails (that suite pins packs at 33d3a430,
~190 commits behind our BASELINE) -- byte-identical failure sets, so this adds
zero regressions. The superseded stack failed 15.

Refs gc-10x, gc-mgn, gc-a6d, gc-p0j, gc-5bc

